{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GQ7WV3dNo4ywAGX6jDBVCb","type":"tool_result","content":"C:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\book-outline\\domain-analysis.md:37:[Omitted long matching line]\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\book-outline\\domain-analysis.md:38:[Omitted long matching line]\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\book-outline\\domain-analysis.md:39:[Omitted long matching line]\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\book-outline\\domain-analysis.md:164:### D2.1 — Networking\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\book-outline\\domain-analysis.md:190:### D2.2 — Security\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\book-outline\\domain-analysis.md:214:### D2.3 — Troubleshooting\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\book-outline\\domain-analysis.md:232:### D2.4 — Storage\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\book-outline\\domain-analysis.md:300:Shares its concept set with **D2.3 Troubleshooting** above, scoped to the application side: Troubleshooting Applications, determining Pod failure reasons, init-container and running-Pod debugging, `kubectl debug`, and developing/debugging services locally.\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\book-outline\\domain-analysis.md:487:4. **Storage (D2.4) must precede StatefulSet's full treatment.** Either introduce StatefulSet lightly in the workloads chapter and return to it after PV/PVC, or place storage before the deep workloads pass. The former is better for the reader — StatefulSet belongs with its siblings.\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\book-outline\\domain-analysis.md:489:6. **NetworkPolicy sits in both D2.1 and D2.2.** Teach it once, in Networking, and cross-bear from the Security chapter. Duplicating it costs words and creates two slightly-divergent explanations.\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\book-outline\\domain-analysis.md:490:7. **Troubleshooting (D2.3) and Debugging (D3.2) should be one teaching arc split across two chapters** by scope: platform-side symptoms in the D2 chapter, application-side workflow in the D3 chapter, with explicit cross-bearings both ways. The exam splits them across domains; the reader's mental model should not be split.\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\book-outline\\domain-analysis.md:600:| 87 | Treating D3.2 Debugging as identical to D2.3 Troubleshooting. | The exam splits them; the split is roughly application-scope vs cluster-scope. A question tagged to one may still test the other's tooling. | [inferred] |\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\book-outline\\domain-analysis.md:644:| G1 | **`kubectl` command surface.** The entire book depends on `kubectl get / describe / logs / exec / events / apply / top / port-forward / debug`, and no cached source documents them. | D1.2, D2.3, D3.2 | kubernetes.io/docs/reference/kubectl/ + the kubectl cheat sheet |\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\book-outline\\domain-analysis.md:645:| G2 | **Pod failure signatures by name.** CrashLoopBackOff, ImagePullBackOff, ErrImagePull, OOMKilled, Evicted, CreateContainerConfigError. Not one appears in the cached set, and these are the single most likely troubleshooting question material. | D2.3, D3.2 | kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/debug/debug-application/debug-pods/ |\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\book-outline\\domain-analysis.md:646:| G3 | **Resource requests and limits, and QoS classes** (Guaranteed / Burstable / BestEffort). Referenced obliquely (PodFitsResources, \"tell Kubernetes how much CPU and memory\") but never defined. Prerequisite for scheduling, autoscaling, and OOMKilled. | D1.3, D1.1, D2.3 | kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/configuration/manage-resources-containers/ |\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\book-outline\\domain-analysis.md:648:| G5 | **Pod Security Standards and Pod Security Admission** (privileged / baseline / restricted) and `securityContext`. The single most conspicuous D2.2 absence — cached security coverage is RBAC and Secrets only. | D2.2 | kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/security/pod-security-standards/ and .../pod-security-admission/ |\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\book-outline\\domain-analysis.md:649:| G6 | **The 4Cs of Cloud Native Security** (Cloud, Cluster, Container, Code). Standard KCNA-level security framing; entirely absent. | D2.2 | kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/security/overview/ |\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\book-outline\\domain-analysis.md:650:| G7 | **ServiceAccounts as a concept.** Referenced in RBAC subjects, Secret types, and the ServiceAccount controller, but never defined as the Pod identity mechanism. | D2.2, D1.1 | kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/security/service-accounts/ |\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\book-outline\\domain-analysis.md:654:| G11 | **CNI, CSI, and CRI as the three pluggable interfaces.** CRI is covered; CNI is implied (\"network plugin\"); CSI is entirely absent. The three-interface story is core cloud-native-architecture framing. | D2.1, D2.4, D1.4, D4.2 | kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/extend-kubernetes/compute-storage-net/ + the CNI and CSI specs |\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\book-outline\\domain-analysis.md:655:| G12 | **Volume types other than PV/PVC**: emptyDir, hostPath, configMap/secret volumes, projected, ephemeral. A large share of real Pod storage and not covered. | D2.4 | kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/storage/volumes/ |\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\book-outline\\domain-analysis.md:656:| G13 | **CoreDNS and DNS for Services and Pods.** The FQDN format is covered via the namespaces page, but CoreDNS as the cluster DNS addon, and Pod DNS records, are not. | D2.1 | kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/dns-pod-service/ |\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\book-outline\\domain-analysis.md:670:| G22 | **Supply-chain security**: SBOM, image signing, sigstore, in-toto, TUF, Harbor, image scanning. in-toto, TUF, and Harbor appear only in the graduated roster; the concepts are unexplained. | D2.2, D1.4 | Project docs; CNCF software supply chain best practices |\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\book-outline\\domain-analysis.md:671:| G23 | **Policy engines**: OPA/Gatekeeper, Kyverno, Falco. Roster-only mentions. Falco (runtime security) in particular is a common KCNA name-recognition target. | D2.2 | Project docs |\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\book-outline\\domain-analysis.md:672:| G24 | **kube-proxy modes** (iptables, IPVS, nftables). kube-proxy's role is covered; its implementation modes are not. | D2.1 | kubernetes.io/docs/reference/networking/virtual-ips/ |\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\book-outline\\domain-analysis.md:673:| G25 | **Gateway API detail.** Recommended over Ingress by the project, but the cached sources give it one sentence. Given that recommendation, the exam may test its existence and basic role. | D2.1 | gateway-api.sigs.k8s.io |\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\book-outline\\domain-analysis.md:674:| G26 | **Node lifecycle operations**: cordon, drain, uncordon; node conditions (Ready, MemoryPressure, DiskPressure, PIDPressure); eviction. Node leases are covered; the operations are not. | D1.2, D2.3 | kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/architecture/nodes/ |\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\book-outline\\domain-analysis.md:678:| G30 | **Sandboxed and alternative runtimes**: gVisor, Kata Containers, and what RuntimeClass is actually used *for*. RuntimeClass is defined; its motivating use case is not. | D1.4, D2.2 | kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/containers/runtime-class/ |\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\book-outline\\chapter-lineup.md:11:Objective identifiers (`D1.1`, `D2.3`, …) are the **Lodestar convention established in B1**. CNCF publishes four domains and twelve named competencies with no numbering and no sub-weights (B1 §G33, §G36). Per-chapter weight figures below are **authored judgment**, not published data, and must be disclosed as such in front matter.\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\book-outline\\chapter-lineup.md:42:| 9 | Every Pod Has an Address | *\"Flat networks, stable names, and the abstraction that makes churn survivable\"* | III | **D2.1** — the Kubernetes network model, Pod IP and shared namespace, CNI, Service, ClusterIP, NodePort, LoadBalancer, ExternalName, headless Services, Services without selectors, EndpointSlice, service proxy, kube-proxy modes, CoreDNS, Service and Pod DNS records, FQDN | 4, 5, 6 | 7 |\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\book-outline\\chapter-lineup.md:43:[Omitted long matching line]\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\book-outline\\chapter-lineup.md:44:| 11 | Below the Waterline | *\"Storage outlives the Pod that asked for it\"* | III | **D2.4** — volume types (emptyDir, hostPath, configMap/secret, projected, ephemeral), PersistentVolume, PersistentVolumeClaim, StorageClass, static vs dynamic provisioning, binding, reclaim policies (Retain/Delete/Recycle), access modes (RWO/ROX/RWX/RWOP), CSI, StatefulSet + PV pairing (loop closed from Ch 6) | 4, 5, 6 | 5 |\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\book-outline\\chapter-lineup.md:45:[Omitted long matching line]\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\book-outline\\chapter-lineup.md:46:| 13 | When the Cluster Won't Answer | *\"Read the phase before you read the logs\"* | III | **D2.3** — the two-audience split, Pod failure signatures (Pending, CrashLoopBackOff, ImagePullBackOff, ErrImagePull, OOMKilled, Evicted, CreateContainerConfigError), events, `kubectl describe`/`events`/`logs --previous`, node health and conditions, crictl, the resource metrics pipeline and metrics-server, `kubectl top`, cluster log architecture, known issues | 5, 7, 9, 11 | 4 |\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\book-outline\\chapter-lineup.md:77:[Omitted long matching line]\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\ch-04\\outline.md:11:exam_domain: \"Kubernetes Fundamentals (competency: Kubernetes Core Concepts)\"\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\book-outline\\arc-outline.md:61:- **Troubleshooting is one arc across two chapters.** The exam splits platform scope (Ch 13, D2.3) from application scope (Ch 16, D3.2); the reader's mental model should not be split. Reciprocal cross-bearings bind them.\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\book-outline\\arc-outline.md:195:- **Purpose**: domain — D2.1 Container orchestration / networking (chapter_type: `content`)\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\book-outline\\arc-outline.md:196:- **Covers**: **D2.1** — the Kubernetes network model; Pod IP and shared namespace; CNI; Service; ClusterIP; NodePort; LoadBalancer; ExternalName; headless Services; Services without selectors; EndpointSlice; the service proxy; kube-proxy modes; CoreDNS; Service and Pod DNS records; FQDN\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\book-outline\\arc-outline.md:210:- **Purpose**: domain — D2.1 (+ D2.2 boundary) / external exposure and network policy (chapter_type: `content`)\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\book-outline\\arc-outline.md:211:- **Covers**: **D2.1** — Ingress; Ingress controllers; TLS termination; name-based virtual hosting; simple fanout; edge router; the Ingress freeze and the Gateway recommendation; Gateway API (GatewayClass / Gateway / HTTPRoute, role-oriented design, request flow); NetworkPolicy (ingress and egress isolation, selectors, ipBlock, additive semantics, plugin dependency, the out-of-scope list)\n\n[Showing results with pagination = limit: 40]"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6cf65a2d-ef33-4d76-94d9-6157e0d58fdc","uuid":"b90b50ad-15e9-4755-8e77-d66a3fee4506","timestamp":"2026-08-31T06:34:48.098Z","tool_use_result":{"mode":"content","numFiles":0,"filenames":[],"content":"C:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\book-outline\\domain-analysis.md:37:[Omitted long matching line]\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\book-outline\\domain-analysis.md:38:[Omitted long matching line]\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\book-outline\\domain-analysis.md:39:[Omitted long matching line]\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\book-outline\\domain-analysis.md:164:### D2.1 — Networking\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\book-outline\\domain-analysis.md:190:### D2.2 — Security\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\book-outline\\domain-analysis.md:214:### D2.3 — Troubleshooting\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\book-outline\\domain-analysis.md:232:### D2.4 — Storage\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\book-outline\\domain-analysis.md:300:Shares its concept set with **D2.3 Troubleshooting** above, scoped to the application side: Troubleshooting Applications, determining Pod failure reasons, init-container and running-Pod debugging, `kubectl debug`, and developing/debugging services locally.\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\book-outline\\domain-analysis.md:487:4. **Storage (D2.4) must precede StatefulSet's full treatment.** Either introduce StatefulSet lightly in the workloads chapter and return to it after PV/PVC, or place storage before the deep workloads pass. The former is better for the reader — StatefulSet belongs with its siblings.\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\book-outline\\domain-analysis.md:489:6. **NetworkPolicy sits in both D2.1 and D2.2.** Teach it once, in Networking, and cross-bear from the Security chapter. Duplicating it costs words and creates two slightly-divergent explanations.\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\book-outline\\domain-analysis.md:490:7. **Troubleshooting (D2.3) and Debugging (D3.2) should be one teaching arc split across two chapters** by scope: platform-side symptoms in the D2 chapter, application-side workflow in the D3 chapter, with explicit cross-bearings both ways. The exam splits them across domains; the reader's mental model should not be split.\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\book-outline\\domain-analysis.md:600:| 87 | Treating D3.2 Debugging as identical to D2.3 Troubleshooting. | The exam splits them; the split is roughly application-scope vs cluster-scope. A question tagged to one may still test the other's tooling. | [inferred] |\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\book-outline\\domain-analysis.md:644:| G1 | **`kubectl` command surface.** The entire book depends on `kubectl get / describe / logs / exec / events / apply / top / port-forward / debug`, and no cached source documents them. | D1.2, D2.3, D3.2 | kubernetes.io/docs/reference/kubectl/ + the kubectl cheat sheet |\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\book-outline\\domain-analysis.md:645:| G2 | **Pod failure signatures by name.** CrashLoopBackOff, ImagePullBackOff, ErrImagePull, OOMKilled, Evicted, CreateContainerConfigError. Not one appears in the cached set, and these are the single most likely troubleshooting question material. | D2.3, D3.2 | kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/debug/debug-application/debug-pods/ |\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\book-outline\\domain-analysis.md:646:| G3 | **Resource requests and limits, and QoS classes** (Guaranteed / Burstable / BestEffort). Referenced obliquely (PodFitsResources, \"tell Kubernetes how much CPU and memory\") but never defined. Prerequisite for scheduling, autoscaling, and OOMKilled. | D1.3, D1.1, D2.3 | kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/configuration/manage-resources-containers/ |\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\book-outline\\domain-analysis.md:648:| G5 | **Pod Security Standards and Pod Security Admission** (privileged / baseline / restricted) and `securityContext`. The single most conspicuous D2.2 absence — cached security coverage is RBAC and Secrets only. | D2.2 | kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/security/pod-security-standards/ and .../pod-security-admission/ |\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\book-outline\\domain-analysis.md:649:| G6 | **The 4Cs of Cloud Native Security** (Cloud, Cluster, Container, Code). Standard KCNA-level security framing; entirely absent. | D2.2 | kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/security/overview/ |\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\book-outline\\domain-analysis.md:650:| G7 | **ServiceAccounts as a concept.** Referenced in RBAC subjects, Secret types, and the ServiceAccount controller, but never defined as the Pod identity mechanism. | D2.2, D1.1 | kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/security/service-accounts/ |\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\book-outline\\domain-analysis.md:654:| G11 | **CNI, CSI, and CRI as the three pluggable interfaces.** CRI is covered; CNI is implied (\"network plugin\"); CSI is entirely absent. The three-interface story is core cloud-native-architecture framing. | D2.1, D2.4, D1.4, D4.2 | kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/extend-kubernetes/compute-storage-net/ + the CNI and CSI specs |\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\book-outline\\domain-analysis.md:655:| G12 | **Volume types other than PV/PVC**: emptyDir, hostPath, configMap/secret volumes, projected, ephemeral. A large share of real Pod storage and not covered. | D2.4 | kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/storage/volumes/ |\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\book-outline\\domain-analysis.md:656:| G13 | **CoreDNS and DNS for Services and Pods.** The FQDN format is covered via the namespaces page, but CoreDNS as the cluster DNS addon, and Pod DNS records, are not. | D2.1 | kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/dns-pod-service/ |\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\book-outline\\domain-analysis.md:670:| G22 | **Supply-chain security**: SBOM, image signing, sigstore, in-toto, TUF, Harbor, image scanning. in-toto, TUF, and Harbor appear only in the graduated roste